WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.219 --> 00:00:05.279
We humans can build some pretty spectacular
things.

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:10.879
Skyscrapers, monuments, and majestic feats
of engineering spring from the ground wherever

00:00:10.879 --> 00:00:13.860
we settle
—as if we just can’t help but shake a

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.760
triumphant fist at the sky before marking
our territory with another piece of architectural

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.760
artwork.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.169
But, did you know that the greatest architects
and designers in the world don’t necessarily

00:00:25.169 --> 00:00:28.060
work at a desk in a fancy office?

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.960
To find them and their awe inspiring projects,
you might have to take a walk on the wild

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:33.960
side.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:42.300
Here are seven amazing structures created
by animals.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.900
In the remote and untamed wilderness area
of Wood Buffalo National Park in Alberta,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.100
Canada,
everyone’s favorite semi-aquatic rodents

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.030
have banded together to earn their very own
world record.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:01:00.900
Since the mid-1970s, generations of beavers
have spent their lives felling trees in preparation

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.390
for their magnum opus—
the World’s Biggest Beaver Dam.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.880
Not to be confused with the quaint little
hamlet of Beaverdam, Alberta.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.800
hough certainly they have just as much to
be proud of.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.890
The impressive structure was first discovered
in 2007 thanks to researchers using Google

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:18.890
Earth.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.220
Yes, the dam is so big it can be seen from
space.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.020
Step aside, Great Wall of China.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:30.850
It wasn’t until 2014 that an adventurous
person made the journey to see it for themselves.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:36.960
Measuring a whopping 850 metres long and formed
from two different dams joined together, it’s

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.479
the length which earns it the title of biggest
in the world.

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:46.299
Two beaver lodges house the industrious workers,
offering a place for them to rest and celebrate

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:47.670
their fine work.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:52.369
Although they’re not interested in resting
for very long as the beavers continue developing

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:56.270
the dam to this day,
building toward another dam under construction

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:02.930
nearby which will add between fifty and one
hundred meters to the dam’s total length.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.310
Like other enthusiastic record holders, they’re
looking to defend their title—and perhaps

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.620
give some human-scale dams a run for their
money.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.849
“Busy as a beaver” indeed.

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:19.690
Equally as renowned as a beaver for his work
ethic, the lowly ant is capable of lifting

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.650
objects over ten times its own weight
and building elaborate communities out of

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.390
the very soil beneath you.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:31.360
We’ve all seen ant hills, the little mounds
of dirt piled ankle high around the entrances

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.890
and exits of ant colonies,
but just how deep do they go, exactly?

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.850
In 2012, the largest known ant city was unearthed
in the country of Brazil, covering an area

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:48.480
of five hundred square feet and plunging twenty-six
feet beneath the surface.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.680
Based on best estimates, it is presumed that
the ants moved nearly forty tons of soil while

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.450
excavating the labyrinthine network of tunnels,
chambers, and other distinct structures.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.650
To put that weight into perspective, it would
be the same as the ants shifting a humpback

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.830
whale bit by bit to build their home.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.940
That’s no small feat!

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.180
When the ant city was discovered, the leafcutter
ants who once called it home had long since

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.740
abandoned it for greener pastures,
though they left behind telltale signs of

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:23.140
how they organized the caste system to which
they were bound.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:28.280
Within the colony were distinct rubbish heaps,
where the refuse generated by the ants would

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.959
have been gathered to be removed by designated
garbage handlers.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:37.560
Gardens were dotted throughout for the cultivation
of the edible fungus that the ants subsisted

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.781
on,
beside major thoroughfares which cut through

00:03:40.781 --> 00:03:43.690
the heart of the colony, just like our highways.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.950
It’s eerily similar to how human society
must look from a bird’s eye view, and surely

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.840
ranks as one of the most spectacular feats
ants have ever accomplished.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.260
So the next time you’ve got a magnifying
glass and you’re feeling antsy,

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.420
perhaps you should consider giving the little
buggers a pass and find something else to

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.760
burn.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.590
Not all termites live in the walls of suburban
homes—though that might be hard to believe

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:09.590
at first.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.629
They would chew their way through a tree just
as soon as they’d cause structural damage

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:17.940
to your house,
but out in the wide open spaces of nature

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:23.320
some termites prefer a little extra shelter
from the elements than a layer of bark.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.919
Enter the termite mound,
towering like a natural Colossus of Rhodes,

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:32.190
and filled to the brim with termite ingenuity
and efficiency.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.840
Reaching heights of up to nine meters, the
termites like to test the limits of their

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.900
natural engineering talent;
though it’s even more impressive when you

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:47.300
consider that for an equivalent human building,
it would be around two kilometers tall!

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:53.800
That’s the same as taking two of Saudi Arabia’s
one kilometer high Kingdom Tower and stacking

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.800
them.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.620
Termites work together as a cohesive, coordinated
unit—though they have no language or method

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.860
of communication other than pheromones and
basic physical interactions.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.300
They are able to sense something as simple
as damage to the outer wall of the mound,

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.660
and within minutes begin to work together
on repairs.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:19.030
If their entire mound were destroyed, it could
be completely rebuilt by the colony within

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:20.720
three months.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:27.430
All without words or blueprints—being that
talented, you’d think termites would be

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.040
esteemed as patron saints of construction
workers everywhere!

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.000
But sadly, the stereotype of termites as vicious
home destroyers lives on.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.550
To help control the temperature and allow
airflow through the colony, ventilation shafts

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.770
are built to draw in fresh air from the outside,
and the entire mound is formed to naturally

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.500
circulate air.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:52.451
When combined with the insulation offered
by the earth around the nest, the termites

00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:57.860
are able to maintain a pleasant temperature
year round with remarkably little temperature

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.300
variation.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.510
Just like the people of Coober Pedy, Australia,
these brilliant little insects must know the

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.220
joys of living entirely underground.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.220
Given an average fifteen kilograms of pure,
unadulterated termites in your average mound,

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:08.220
you can assume they will move approximately
one quarter of a metric ton of dirt in an

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.220
average year as they build and maintain their
homes.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:13.540
Just like ants, termites burrow into the soil,
termites however, choose to build higher instead

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:16.789
of deeper,
creating monuments which will last at least

00:06:16.789 --> 00:06:21.080
until the elements or a malicious predator
brings them down.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:25.860
Off the coast of Japan in the mid-nineties,
scuba divers discovered mysterious patterns

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.340
in the silt and sand of the ocean floor.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:33.490
Scientists were completely baffled—there
were no obvious explanations for the undersea

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:38.440
crop circles that kept appearing, at least
at first.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:44.750
They watched and waited for a decade, trying
to catch the creator in the act, but they

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.110
saw nothing.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:51.990
It wasn’t until they decided to take note
of the unassuming pufferfish that they finally

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.630
had their culprit.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:59.180
No more than twelve centimeters long, the
tiny pufferfish shape curious circles on the

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.520
seafloor of up to two meters in diameter.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.830
Although no one is exactly certain why, the
best guess of well-educated men and women

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:12.100
is that the circles are used as a part of
the mating ritual between male and female

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.100
pufferfish.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:18.949
Instead of a metal ring, the male pufferfish
carves a ring in the rapidly shifting sands

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:23.650
beneath the surf, and proudly presents it
on bended fin to his beloved.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:27.639
Actually, that’s rather romantic when you
look at it that way.

00:07:27.639 --> 00:07:33.539
Female pufferfish lay their eggs in the fine
sediment at the center before turning tail

00:07:33.539 --> 00:07:39.300
leaving the males to guard the fertilised
eggs for another six days afterward.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.230
Counting the week or so it takes the male
to make the damn thing, that means that they

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:50.240
spend two weeks on something the females use
only for a day or two at best.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.240
That’s rough.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:57.000
The circles, while beautiful, are not actively
maintained by the pufferfish, and will wash

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.320
away soon after their usefulness has ended.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.460
However, their design takes into account the
constant ebb and flow of underwater currents,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:10.560
with fluid dynamics tests finding that the
way they are shaped actually serves to slow

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.060
the currents as they near the center of the
circle.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:19.370
This serves to ensure that the softest sand
at the center, reserved for the eggs, lasts

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:24.270
as long as it needs to, undisturbed by the
undertow of the ocean.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:30.259
Each circle is a temporary and delicate work
of art which can hold back the sea itself

00:08:30.259 --> 00:08:35.779
in order to provide the best conditions for
the next generation of pufferfish.

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:41.519
Pufferfish are therefore, living proof, that
spectacular things can come from the smallest

00:08:41.519 --> 00:08:42.519
packages.

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:48.589
In 2010, after an exceptionally intense monsoon
season, northeastern Pakistan experienced

00:08:48.589 --> 00:08:53.529
widespread flooding that covered nearly one
fifth of its total land area.

00:08:53.529 --> 00:08:58.079
Many of the frontier provinces suffered as
a result, with more than twenty million people

00:08:58.079 --> 00:09:01.239
displaced as a result of the surging floodwaters.

00:09:01.239 --> 00:09:05.369
But humans weren’t the only ones driven
from their homes.

00:09:05.369 --> 00:09:11.649
Seemingly overnight, the trees in flooded
areas were completely cocooned in spiderwebs.

00:09:11.649 --> 00:09:17.310
Yes, that’s right—spiders fled the waters
as well, seeking the highest and driest places

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:18.449
available to them.

00:09:18.449 --> 00:09:23.369
In short order, the foliage of eastern Pakistan
took on the appearance of elaborately wrapped

00:09:23.369 --> 00:09:27.140
Egyptian mummies,
and lots of people who didn’t know they

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:32.519
suffered from arachnophobia figured it out
pretty darn quickly.

00:09:32.519 --> 00:09:38.290
White frosted branches as far as the eye can
see—it’s not quite the hell on earth you

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:39.660
might be imagining.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:45.429
The creepy crawlies spinning webs in the tree
tops may have actually provided a small measure

00:09:45.429 --> 00:09:49.089
of disaster relief for the stricken Pakistani
people.

00:09:49.089 --> 00:09:53.899
Despite the vast new plots of standing and
stagnant water, there was no complimentary

00:09:53.899 --> 00:10:01.190
rise in malaria infections due to what should
have been a booming mosquito population.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:06.889
The best guess is that the spiders were able
to effectively control the mosquitos and prevent

00:10:06.889 --> 00:10:10.610
them from running rampant across the saturated
countryside.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:14.769
Not quite the evil eight-legged abominations
you took them for, eh?

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:20.079
Flooding in Pakistan continues to this day,
so for now the spiders continue to weave their

00:10:20.079 --> 00:10:22.399
spell over the region.

00:10:22.399 --> 00:10:27.559
There is some evidence that the webbing covering
the leaves may be having a detrimental effect

00:10:27.559 --> 00:10:33.030
on the trees, but there simply aren’t any
volunteers to clear out the infestation.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.850
As long as they’re keeping malaria-ridden
mosquitos at bay, I suspect the Pakistani

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:41.509
people will continue to enforce the current
status quo between themselves and their new

00:10:41.509 --> 00:10:42.509
neighbors.

00:10:42.509 --> 00:10:47.689
It’s a well-known fact that birds prefer
to situate their nests up and away from prying

00:10:47.689 --> 00:10:50.639
eyes and thieving paws.

00:10:50.639 --> 00:10:54.199
Most are content to gather twigs together
in a fork of the branches where they can settle

00:10:54.199 --> 00:10:58.370
down and perhaps lay an egg or two in relative
obscurity.

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:03.620
Not so for the Montezuma Oropendola birds
of Central America—who prefer a flashier,

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:07.029
yet isolated lifestyle.

00:11:07.029 --> 00:11:11.389
The magnificent, basket-like hanging nests
of the Montezuma Oropendola are often seen

00:11:11.389 --> 00:11:14.720
in single trees set apart from the rest of
the forest.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:19.660
The peculiar birds prefer solitary bases from
which to hang their homes, often choosing

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:24.730
trees near wasps’ nests or other hazards
so as to deter potential predators.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:29.389
They’re the very poster child of introversion
in the animal kingdom.

00:11:29.389 --> 00:11:33.709
You can almost hear them saying: “I built
a pretty awesome nest, but don’t bother

00:11:33.709 --> 00:11:35.110
coming to see it.”

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:40.279
They thrive in the tropical climate around
Nicaragua, northwestern Costa Rica, and parts

00:11:40.279 --> 00:11:42.610
of Panama and southern Mexico.

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:48.459
Like most creatures native to the region,
they have no qualms about putting on a display—as

00:11:48.459 --> 00:11:52.839
evidenced by their conspicuous nests and bright,
ruddy plumage.

00:11:52.839 --> 00:11:55.269
Look but don’t approach is their creed.

00:11:55.269 --> 00:12:01.049
And if you prefer not to tango with wasps,
you would be wise to heed their warning.

00:12:01.049 --> 00:12:06.779
North and East of Australia, just off the
coast, lies the largest structure made by

00:12:06.779 --> 00:12:07.989
living organisms.

00:12:07.989 --> 00:12:12.699
It’s far larger than the World’s Biggest
Beaver Dam, and of course, it’s visible

00:12:12.699 --> 00:12:14.089
from space as well.

00:12:14.089 --> 00:12:18.769
Yes, I’m talking about the Great Barrier
Reef, one of the wonders of the natural world

00:12:18.769 --> 00:12:23.110
and home to countless types of fish and other
assorted aquatic creatures.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:29.410
A gorgeous stretch of ocean which spans more
than 344,000 square kilometers—it covers

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:34.240
an area larger than the United Kingdom, Holland,
and Switzerland combined!

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:39.519
It was officially recognized and protected
as the Great Barrier Reef Park by the Australian

00:12:39.519 --> 00:12:42.360
government in 1975.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:47.540
With the protected status came strict rules
and regulations against human interference,

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:51.970
providing a unique shelter for migrating whales
and endangered species like the dugong and

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.269
the Large Green Sea Turtle.

00:12:54.269 --> 00:12:58.029
Thanks to the efforts of conservationists,
the Great Barrier Reef is still a pristine

00:12:58.029 --> 00:13:03.259
refuge for thousands of ocean dwelling species
in a world where the oceans seem to grow more

00:13:03.259 --> 00:13:05.720
crowded with each passing decade.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:10.420
Though photographs and popular culture might
have you believe that the entire reef is comprised

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:17.769
of coral, in fact only around 7% of it is
actually made of the colorful “reef builders”.

00:13:17.769 --> 00:13:23.259
It is, however, the largest collection of
corals in the world—giving it the distinction

00:13:23.259 --> 00:13:27.880
of also being the largest living structure,
in a sense.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:33.679
Besides the coral reefs, other types of marine
habitats like shallow seagrass fields share

00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:38.430
the space beside algal and sponge gardens,
just to name a few.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:44.160
It’s a flourishing ecosystem, and a priceless
investment for scientists and researchers

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:50.749
who study our oceans to find better ways for
us to coexist.

